 ## Overview
 
-The Blinn-Phong shading model is a local illumination model that approximates the reflected light intensity from a surface to modulate color, typically derived from a transfer function in volume rendering [S1, book][S8]. It computes specular highlights using a cosine-power lobe based on the half-vector between the light and view directions, distinguishing it from the original Phong model which uses the reflection vector [S2, S3, S6, unofficial]. Unlike global illumination methods, it ignores indirect light, shadows, and other global effects, relying solely on local surface properties and light source parameters [S1, book][S8].
