 ## Background
 
-The Blinn-Phong shading model was introduced by James F. Blinn as an approximation to the Phong reflection model, offering a computationally cheaper way to calculate specular highlights by using the half-vector instead of the reflection vector [S1, book][S8]. It has become one of the most common local illumination models in computer graphics, particularly in real-time rendering and volume visualization [S1, book][S8]. In volume rendering, it is frequently modified to handle the unique challenge of estimating surface normals from scalar fields, often by restricting shading to regions with high gradient magnitudes [S1, book][S8]. The model is implemented in various graphics libraries and shaders, such as the `glsl-specular-blinn-phong` module [S2, unofficial].
